xt":"95% range +4.1 to +10.5","display":"+7.5","p":0.0008,"q":0.003,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":10,"label":"Oct","name":"October","sen_per_decade":8.22,"ci95":[6.039,10.534],"ci_text":"95% range +6.0 to +10.6","display":"+8.2","p":0.0,"q":0.0004,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":11,"label":"Nov","name":"November","sen_per_decade":8.52,"ci95":[4.085,13.138],"ci_text":"95% range +4.0 to +13.2","display":"+8.5","p":0.0044,"q":0.0071,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":12,"label":"Dec","name":"December","sen_per_decade":7.67,"ci95":[2.447,12.56],"ci_text":"95% range +2.4 to +12.6","display":"+7.7","p":0.0092,"q":0.0113,"passes_fdr":true}],"extremes":null,"spatial":{"share_opposite_significant":0.015,"share_same_significant":0.615,"warn":false},"neighbour":null,"insights":[{"kind":"total_change","text":"Since 2003 the water stored on land got about 15.7 cm higher.","detail":"Total change +15.7 cm (95% range +10.1 to +21.1).","evidence_level":"strong","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"trend-test","numbers":{"total":15.69}},{"kind":"signal_noise","text":"Since about 2007 the change has been bigger than a normal year-to-year swing.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"signal-noise","numbers":{"year":2007,"ratio_now":7.34}},{"kind":"season_unresolved","text":"Autumn shows the fastest gain in stored water, but the seasons are still too close to tell apart.","detail":"Autumn: +8.0 cm per decade; the other seasons +5.8 to +7.1. Their 95% ranges overlap.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"seasons","numbers":{"fast":8.03}}],"insights_audit":{"tested":16,"passed":16,"shown":0,"q":0.05,"text":"Besides the main test, we ran 16 follow-up checks (12 months and 4 seasons). 16 held up after correcting for running many tests at once (false discovery rate q = 0.05); most of them repeat the main trend month by month. Findings marked “follow-up check” come from these checks."},"deeper_checks":null,"provenance":{"dataset":"NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO (JPL mascons) · lwe_thickness","version":"RL06.3Mv04","doi":"10.5067/TEMSC-3JC634","landing_page":"https://podaac.jpl.nasa.gov/dataset/TELLUS_GRAC-GRFO_MASCON_CRI_GRID_RL06.3_V4","accessed":"2026-11-14","processing":["converted longitudes 0..360 to -180..180","kept land cells only (product land_mask == 1)","region value = mean of the 2° map squares that overlap the region's outline, weighted by the overlapping area","monthly series 2003-01 to 2025-12 (complete years)","anomalies from the calendar-month normal; Sen's slope, Mann-Kendall (Yue-Wang), OLS with effective sample size (trend_stats.py)"],"synthetic":false,"caveat":"Data: NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO satellites, which weigh the water on land by its gravity; JPL mascon solution RL06.3, blocks of about 300 km. The trend is a straight line through 2003–2025; it shows what changed, not why. The record is 23 years long, shorter than the 30 years usually used to describe a climate. GRACE weighs all water together (it cannot say whether groundwater, soil or snow changed), sees blocks of about 300 km, and has no data from mid-2017 to mid-2018 between the two missions; months without data are left out, never filled."}}